The Solar System

The Solar System consists of the Sun and everything that orbits around it, including
eight planets, their moons, dwarf planets, asteroids, and comets.

The Sun is a star at the center of the Solar System. It contains about 99.8% of the
Solar System's total mass and provides the light and heat needed for life on Earth.

The eight planets, in order from the Sun, are: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter,
Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune. Mercury is the smallest and closest planet to the Sun.
Venus is the hottest planet due to its thick, greenhouse-effect-heavy atmosphere.

Earth is the only known planet to support life. It has one natural satellite, the Moon.

Mars is often called the "Red Planet" because of the iron oxide on its surface. It has
two small moons, Phobos and Deimos, and is a major target for future human exploration.

Jupiter is the largest planet in the Solar System. It is a gas giant with a famous
storm called the Great Red Spot, which has been raging for hundreds of years.

Saturn is best known for its beautiful ring system, made mostly of ice particles with
some rocky debris and dust.

Uranus and Neptune are ice giants. Uranus rotates on its side, unlike any other planet,
and Neptune has the strongest winds in the Solar System, reaching up to 2,100 km/h.

Beyond Neptune lies the Kuiper Belt, home to dwarf planets like Pluto, which was
reclassified from a full planet to a dwarf planet in 2006 by the International
Astronomical Union.
